     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "I've marked the run anomalous: tube pressure has been rising steadily during growth, and the cause is in the instrument, though I can't yet say which part.\n\n- **Pressure:** it stayed flat at about 2.95–2.98 Torr through the whole ramp. Since growth started at t=2520 s it has climbed every reading, from 2.952 to 3.159 Torr (about 7%). That's far bigger than the normal jitter of about 0.015 Torr. No normal-run reference ranges came with these events, so I'm judging only against this run's own baseline.\n- **What's normal:** argon flow held at about 100 sccm, so the flow isn't driving the rise. Temperature settled at the 750 °C setpoint after a small overshoot, and heater power looks normal.\n- **Likely causes:** a seal leak letting air in, or an exhaust blockage. The furnace has no exhaust O2 sensor, so I can't tell them apart, and I can't confirm the growth atmosphere either. That sensor is one the growth stage is supposed to have.\n- **Sample:** there's no characterization yet. If the atmosphere turns out to have been compromised, any result from this run shouldn't count as evidence about the recipe.\n\nI've proposed calling a human. Either an O2 reading or a leak check on the tube would show which cause it is."
     }
